          "path": "_m/x/n/7/3/tokyo-bay-aqua-line-wp/tokyo-bay-aqua-line-al.jpg",
          "filename": "Tokyo Wan Aqua-Line.jpg",
          "caption": "The Tokyo Bay Aqua-Line, also known as the Trans Tokyo Bay Expressway, is a part of Route 409, which connects Kawasaki City in Kanagawa Prefecture and Kisarazu City, in Chiba Prefecture. The expressway consists of a 9.5-km undersea tunnel that starts in Kawasaki, a 4.4-km bridge from Kisarazu, two man-made islands, and additional access roads.[1] Two columns of vehicles merged on a 5-level commercial facility and parking spaces of Umihotaru, built on a 650m Kisarazu artificial island with a parking space overlooking Tokyo Bay 360 degrees.[2] The driver can cross Tokyo Bay in about 15 minutes. Tokyo Bay Aqua-Line is the second-longest undersea road tunnel in the world[3] after The Ryfast Tunnel.[4] 2013–2020",
          "alt": "The Tokyo Bay Aqua-Line, also known as the Trans Tokyo Bay Expressway, is a part of Route 409, which connects Kawasaki City in Kanagawa Prefecture and Kisarazu City, in Chiba Prefecture. The expressway consists of a 9.5-km undersea tunnel that starts in Kawasaki, a 4.4-km bridge from Kisarazu, two man-made islands, and additional access roads.[1] Two columns of vehicles merged on a 5-level commercial facility and parking spaces of Umihotaru, built on a 650m Kisarazu artificial island with a parking space overlooking Tokyo Bay 360 degrees.[2] The driver can cross Tokyo Bay in about 15 minutes. Tokyo Bay Aqua-Line is the second-longest undersea road tunnel in the world[3] after The Ryfast Tunnel.[4] 2013–2020",
          "width": 5152,
          "height": 3864,
